@node CMacs AI Providers
@section Providers

Nine providers are wired through the same @code{cmacs-ai-client-new}
DEFUN:

@table @code
@item claude
Anthropic Claude (HTTP).  Reads @env{ANTHROPIC_API_KEY} or
@env{CLAUDE_API_KEY}.
@item openai
OpenAI GPT (HTTP).  Reads @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}.
@item gemini
Google Gemini (HTTP).  Reads @env{GEMINI_API_KEY}.
@item grok
xAI Grok (HTTP).  Reads @env{XAI_API_KEY} or @env{GROK_API_KEY}.
@item ollama
Ollama (local HTTP).  Reads @env{OLLAMA_HOST}.
@item claude-code
The @command{claude} CLI in @option{--print} mode.
@item opencode
The @command{opencode} CLI.
@item claude-tmux
@command{claude} driven via an ephemeral tmux session.
@item grok-build
xAI's @command{grok} CLI in headless mode.  Reads @env{GROK_PATH} to
locate the binary.
@end table

@code{grok} and @code{grok-build} are two different backends, not two
names for one.  @code{grok} is xAI's HTTP API and takes API model ids;
@code{grok-build} wraps the agentic CLI and takes @emph{its} model ids
(@samp{grok-4.6}, @samp{grok-4.5}), which the API rejects and vice
versa.  Run @command{grok models} to see what the installed CLI offers.

Three things about @code{grok-build} differ from the other CLI
providers:

@itemize @bullet
@item
It carries no @code{mcp-config-path}, so
@code{cmacs-ai-client-set-mcp-config} returns @code{nil} for it and a
grok-build agent gets no cmacs tools---only grok's own built-ins.
(@code{opencode} is the same.)
@item
Skipping permissions on it emits @option{--permission-mode
bypassPermissions}, which also lifts grok's sandbox.  The client's
working directory is then the only bound on what it can reach.
@item
@code{max-tokens} does nothing: @command{grok} has no such flag.  Bound
a run with a smaller model instead.
@end itemize

@subsection A local model through the claude CLI

@code{claude-code} and @code{claude-tmux} treat a model beginning with
@samp{ollama/} as a request to use Ollama as the @emph{transport}:
ai-glib spawns

@example
ollama launch claude --model NAME -- @var{the claude arguments}
@end example

rather than @command{claude} directly, omitting claude's own
@option{--model} because Ollama supplies it.  Answer the model prompt
with @samp{ollama/qwen3.5:9b} after choosing @code{claude-code}, or write
the pair as one string wherever a @samp{PROVIDER/MODEL} is taken:

@example
claude-code/ollama/qwen3.5:9b
@end example

This is a different thing from the @code{ollama} provider, which talks
HTTP to the Ollama server with no CLI in the picture.  Use the CLI form
when you want that harness around a model that never leaves the machine;
use the plain @code{ollama} provider when you just want the model.

The claude CLI must still be installed, and @env{OLLAMA_PATH} overrides
the launcher binary.  @samp{ai -p claude-code -m ollama/NAME --dry-run
"hi"} prints the exact command without running it.

@node CMacs AI Chat Buffers
@section Chat Buffers

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-chat} opens a fresh chat buffer in
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-mode} (derived from @code{org-mode}).  The
buffer layout mirrors @code{cmacs-libreclaw-room-mode}:

@example
#+TITLE: cmacs-ai -- 2026-05-25 18:00:00
#+PROPERTY: provider claude

* Conversation
** 18:00:00  user
What is GLib?
** 18:00:00  assistant
GLib is a low-level C utility library...
* Compose                                              :compose:
@end example

History above the @code{* Compose} sentinel is read-only; the
region below is the editable prompt area.  @kbd{C-c C-c} sends the
compose body and streams the response into a fresh @code{** assistant}
heading via the @code{AiStreamable} signal pipeline.

@subsection One heading per exchange

What you send produces exactly one @code{** TIMESTAMP provider/model}
heading, however many times the model stops to call a tool.  Tool calls
and results nest under it as @code{***} entries, and what the model says
afterwards continues beneath.

The heading is opened by the model's first @emph{text}, not by the
stream.  A segment that only calls a tool has nothing to show, and
opening its heading up front is what littered a transcript with empty
ones---a bootstrap reading six files produced seven headings, most
blank.

Two consequences for code written against this:
@code{cmacs-ai-chat--assistant-marker} is nil in the gaps between the
streams of a tool loop, so it means ``no segment is appending'', not
``the chat is idle''; @code{cmacs-ai-chat--turn-open} is the flag that
spans the whole exchange, and anything asking whether the chat is busy
has to read that one or it will deliver into the middle of a turn.

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Send the compose region.
@item C-c C-k
Cancel an in-flight request.
@item C-c C-l
Clear conversation history (server-side and visible).
@item C-c C-s
Archive the chat history to @code{cmacs-ai-chat-dir} (the editable
@code{* Compose} region is excluded).  Always writes, regardless of
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-autosave}.
@item C-c C-o
Resume a saved chat (@code{cmacs-ai-chat-resume}): reopen its
transcript and rebuild the ai-glib session so it continues with full
context.
@item C-c C-a
Attach the buffers you have on screen (@code{cmacs-ai-view-attach}).
See @ref{CMacs AI View}.
@end table

@subsection Why the transcript cannot be edited

A read-only region in Emacs has two ways to fail @emph{open} ---
protecting nothing while looking protected --- and cmacs hit both.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Emacs clears @code{before-change-functions} when a function on
it signals.}  A guard that signals @code{text-read-only} therefore
protects a buffer @emph{exactly once}: after the first stray keypress
the hook is gone and every edit after it lands.  That is what the
transcript guard used to be on its own.

@item
@strong{@code{(read-only t rear-nonsticky t)} --- the comint idiom ---
permits insertion in the MIDDLE of the run.}  With @code{rear-nonsticky}
Emacs decides inserted text would join neither neighbouring interval and
allows the insert, so only the run's edges were protected.
@end enumerate

So finished text carries @code{(read-only t front-sticky t)}, applied at
turn boundaries; the property is enforced in C and cannot be disarmed.
The change guard is kept for the tail of a turn still streaming, and is
re-armed every time the transcript is sealed, so a clearing heals itself
at the next turn rather than lasting the session.

Both are covered by tests that try the same edit three times and once in
the middle --- a single attempt at the edge passes against either bug.

@subsection Saving and Resuming

Chats are archived automatically, mirroring how libreclaw persists
its rooms.  When @code{cmacs-ai-chat-autosave} is non-nil (the
default), a chat is written to @code{cmacs-ai-chat-dir} at three
points: right after you send a message, after each assistant response
completes, and when the buffer is killed --- so a conversation
survives a crash, a cancel, or an accidental close.  The archive
holds the conversation history (preamble plus the turns above
@code{* Compose}); the editable compose region is excluded.  The file
name comes from @code{cmacs-ai-chat-save-name-format} (default
@code{%y%m%d-%H%M%S-<provider>.org}, e.g.@:
@file{260605-142345-claude.org}).

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-resume-chat} (or @kbd{C-c C-o} from a chat buffer)
reopens an archived chat: it restores the transcript @emph{and}
rebuilds the ai-glib session by replaying the visible turns, so the
model keeps full prior context and further messages append to the
same file.  Reopening an already-resumed chat just switches to it.

Two things are intentionally not restored: tool-call / tool-result
blocks are replayed as plain text turns only (the rebuilt session
carries the conversation, not the verbatim tool I/O --- this avoids
provider errors from an orphaned @code{tool_result}); and each chat
is its own archive file (there is no per-project single-file model
like libreclaw's cmacs channel).

@node CMacs AI View
@section What the Model Can See

@cindex screen context
@cindex what I am looking at

A chat buffer is the one place in cmacs where ``here'' is ambiguous.
Ask @emph{please review the first paragraph here} and you mean the file
in the window next door --- but point is in the chat, so everything
that resolves context from point resolves to the conversation instead.

So a chat is told what else is on your screen.

@subsection It is a hint, not a payload

The obvious implementation attaches the visible buffers' text to every
message.  That is wrong twice over: you pay for it on every turn rather
than once, and it is usually unnecessary, because a model with cmacs's
MCP surface can read any buffer it likes.

What is sent instead is a standing instruction in the system prompt ---
a generic reference means the visible buffers, here is how to read them
--- and, on each turn, a short @dfn{inventory}:

@example
[cmacs: on the user's screen right now]
1. cmacs-ai-view.el | ~/src/cmacs/lisp/cmacs/cmacs-ai-view.el |
   emacs-lisp-mode | 457 lines | point on line 212 | showing lines 190-243
2. *shell* | shell-mode | 84 lines | point on line 84 | showing lines 61-84
@end example

Names, files, modes, where point is, which lines are on screen, and
whether a region is highlighted.  A few hundred bytes, whatever the
files weigh.  The model reads what it actually needs with
@code{get_buffer_content}, @code{search_buffer}, or
@code{current_view}.

Neither half appears in the chat buffer.  The transcript shows what you
typed, the same way @code{cmacs-ai-pre-prompt} and the bootstrap
directive already work.

@subsection The one thing that will confuse you

@strong{A hint to ``read it with your tools'' is worthless where there
are no tools.}  So there are two shapes, chosen per chat:

@multitable @columnfractions .48 .16 .36
@headitem Surface @tab Mode @tab What is sent
@item A chat with a tool executor (the default)
  @tab @code{tools} @tab the inventory; the model fetches
@item A CLI agent (claude-code, claude-tmux, opencode)
  @tab @code{tools} @tab ditto, over its own MCP config
@item @code{cmacs-ai-chat-enable-tools} set to nil
  @tab @code{inline} @tab the inventory @emph{and} the text
@item An @option{--without-cmacs-mcp} build
  @tab @code{inline} @tab ditto
@end multitable

@code{inline} mode sends the buffers themselves, clipped to
@code{cmacs-ai-view-inline-max-chars}.  It is the fallback, not the
default, and it is deduplicated: an unchanged screen collapses to one
line rather than being re-sent.

The mode is worked out from the chat, not configured --- see
@code{cmacs-ai-chat--view-mode}.  If a model keeps telling you it
cannot see your screen, that is the thing to check.

@subsection Only what changed

The inventory rides every turn, but only says anything new when there
is something new.  The screen is fingerprinted by window layout, point,
and each buffer's @code{buffer-chars-modified-tick}; an unchanged
fingerprint produces one line saying so.

Editing a visible buffer counts as a change even when the listing would
read identically --- otherwise the model reasons about a version of the
file that no longer exists.

@subsection What is never context

The AI surfaces themselves.  A chat that listed itself would answer
questions about your question, so @code{cmacs-ai-view-exclude-modes}
drops chat buffers, result windows, the harness, libreclaw rooms and
the brigade dashboard, and @code{cmacs-ai-view-exclude-name-regexps}
drops internal buffers by name.  Both are lists you can add your own
surface to; modes are matched with @code{derived-mode-p}, so naming a
parent covers its children.

The buffer the command was invoked from is dropped too --- it is the
chat asking the question.  With nothing else on screen at all (a chat
filling a laptop frame, which is when you are @emph{most} likely to say
``here''), the last non-excluded buffer you selected is used instead.

@subsection Forcing it: C-c C-a

@code{cmacs-ai-view-attach} puts the visible buffers' actual text into
the compose area, as an Org example block: @emph{visible}, so you can
see what you are about to send; @emph{editable}, so you can cut it down
first; and part of the transcript, so a saved chat still makes sense
later.

Each buffer goes through @code{cmacs-ai-target-at}, so this inherits
every resolver the right-click menu has: a highlighted region beats the
whole buffer, and a mail message, a terminal's last command, an Org
subtree, a dired selection or a diff hunk are each understood as
themselves.  With a prefix argument you pick which visible buffers to
attach.

@kbd{C-c C-a} shadows @code{org-attach} in chat and room buffers.  That
is deliberate: in a chat, ``attach'' means the thing you are looking
at.

@subsection What this sends, and how to stop it

Worth being plain about, because it is on by default: the names and
file paths of every buffer you have on screen go to the provider with
every message.  @kbd{C-c C-a} sends their contents.  In @code{inline}
mode the contents go automatically.

@lisp
(setq cmacs-ai-view-attach-mode 'off)  ; C-c C-a still works
@end lisp

@multitable @columnfractions .42 .12 .46
@headitem Variable @tab Default @tab What
@item @code{cmacs-ai-view-attach-mode} @tab @code{hint}
  @tab @code{hint} or @code{off}
@item @code{cmacs-ai-view-exclude-modes} @tab (list)
  @tab modes never reported
@item @code{cmacs-ai-view-exclude-name-regexps} @tab (list)
  @tab buffer names never reported
@item @code{cmacs-ai-view-max-buffers} @tab 8
  @tab most buffers listed; the rest are counted
@item @code{cmacs-ai-view-inline-max-chars} @tab 12000
  @tab budget for the @code{inline} fallback
@end multitable

@subsection Elsewhere

The same layer serves libreclaw's rooms (embedded and remote) and the
@code{current_view} MCP tool, so an agent talking to cmacs over any of
the three gets the same answer to ``what is the user looking at''.

@node CMacs AI Region Commands
@section Region Commands

Acting on the active region without opening a chat buffer:

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-ai-rewrite-region
Prompt for an instruction; replace the region with the AI rewrite.
The current major mode is conveyed so the output uses the right
syntax.  Wrapped in one undo step.
@item M-x cmacs-ai-explain-region
Pop a side window with an Org-mode explanation of the region.
@item M-x cmacs-ai-doc-region
Insert an AI-written doc comment above the region (idiom matches
the language: docstring for Python/Elisp, @code{/** */} for
JavaDoc/Doxygen, etc.).
@item M-x cmacs-ai-test-region
Synthesize a unit test in a scratch buffer.
@end table

A right-click context menu entry is installed via
@code{cmacs-ai-context-menu-install}.

@node CMacs AI Send
@section Sending From the Buffer

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-send} is the command for asking a question where you
are already writing.  It takes the instruction at point, sends it, and
writes the answer back into the same buffer underneath it --- the
in-place equivalent of gptel's @code{gptel-send}.

@subsection What gets sent

The @emph{instruction} is deliberately small and local: the active
region, else --- per @code{cmacs-ai-send-instruction-unit} --- the line
at point, the paragraph, or everything from the start of the buffer to
point.  A blank line falls back to the paragraph above it, so pressing
the key on the empty line under a question does what you meant.

The @emph{document} is sent separately as labelled context, with
@code{cmacs-ai-send-insertion-marker} substituted at the exact position
the answer will be inserted, so the model is told where it is writing
rather than left to infer it.  Buffers over
@code{cmacs-ai-send-context-max-chars} are windowed @emph{around} that
position (plus @code{cmacs-ai-send-context-head-chars} from the head of
the file, which is where the includes and the title live).  Set
@code{cmacs-ai-send-context} to @code{none} to send only the
instruction.

@subsection Answering in the file's own notation

The buffer's format is resolved to one of three profiles, which supplies
both the delimiters written around the answer and the format contract
given to the model:

@table @asis
@item markup
Org and Markdown.  The answer uses that markup --- @code{#+BEGIN_SRC}
and @code{*bold*} in Org, fences and @code{**bold**} in Markdown --- and
emits no headings at all.  That last part matters more than it sounds:
the reply lands in the @emph{middle} of a document, so a single
@samp{* Title} re-parents every heading below the insertion point.  The
model is told to label a section with a bolded line instead.  Set
@code{cmacs-ai-send-allow-headings} to @code{t} to permit headings, in
which case they are held to one level below the heading the answer lands
under.
@item source
Any mode with a comment syntax.  The answer must be @emph{valid code}:
bare code, with all prose written as comments in that language's syntax.
Markdown fences are forbidden by name, because in a @file{.c} file they
are a syntax error.
@item prose
Buffers with no comment syntax (@code{text-mode}, mail).  Plain text.
@end table

The source profile is derived from @code{comment-start} and
@code{comment-end}, so a language cmacs has never heard of is handled
correctly with no entry anywhere: C gets @code{/* @dots{} */}, Python
and shell get @code{#}, Emacs Lisp gets @code{;;}.  Point inside an Org
@code{#+begin_src c} block resolves to @emph{C}, not Org.

The format is stated @emph{twice}: as a contract in the system prompt,
and as a one-line reminder appended after the instruction in every user
message.  The duplication is deliberate --- a system prompt alone does
not reliably hold a model to Org, which is the same finding that made
@code{cmacs-ai-pre-prompt} a per-turn pre-prompt rather than part of
@code{cmacs-ai-system-prompt}.  Both are customisable per profile
(@code{cmacs-ai-send-org-contract}, @code{cmacs-ai-send-org-reminder},
and the same pair for the other formats).

When editing them, write the contract @emph{in} the notation it demands.
The Org contract shipped briefly with its own emphasis in Markdown, and
models answered Org buffers in Markdown accordingly.

Add or override a format with
@code{cmacs-ai-send-register-format} (@code{:name}, @code{:modes},
@code{:predicate}, @code{:order}, @code{:build}); @code{:build} returns a
@code{cmacs-ai-send-format} struct or nil to decline.

@subsection Delimiters, history and regeneration

Responses are wrapped in visible, format-appropriate delimiters: an Org
special block, an HTML comment in Markdown, an ordinary comment in
source.

@example
What is a monoid?

#+begin_ai claude/claude-opus-5
A set with an associative operation and an identity element.
#+end_ai
@end example

Because they survive a save, they are what the rest of the feature is
built on.  @kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-send-delete-response} and @kbd{M-x
cmacs-ai-send-regenerate} find the block around or before point; and in
Org and Markdown, @code{cmacs-ai-send-history} replays the document as a
conversation --- prose between blocks is read as your turns, block
contents as the model's --- so a @file{.org} file behaves like a real
chat with no state stored anywhere but the file you are editing.  When
history applies the document is @emph{not} also sent as context; it is
already in the replayed turns.  The current turn is everything typed
since the last response --- unless a region is active, which is an
explicit statement of scope and is sent as the turn instead.

History stays off in source buffers regardless: the code between two
answers is not a conversation.

@subsection Keys and options

The whole family lives in @code{cmacs-ai-menu-command-map} alongside
the textops commands, so one binding covers everything.  With the map
on @kbd{C-c a}:

@table @kbd
@item C-c a RET
@code{cmacs-ai-send} --- send the thing at point, answer lands here.
@item C-c a g
@code{cmacs-ai-send-regenerate} --- delete the block at point, ask again.
@item C-c a x
@code{cmacs-ai-send-delete-response} --- delete the block at point.
@item C-c a k
@code{cmacs-ai-send-cancel} --- cancel everything streaming here.
@end table

The map itself is not bound anywhere by default.  A direct binding
worth having as well:

@lisp
(when IS-CMACS-AI
  (global-set-key (kbd "C-c C-<return>") #'cmacs-ai-send)
  (global-set-key (kbd "C-c C-M-<return>") #'cmacs-ai-send-regenerate))
@end lisp

With a prefix argument @code{cmacs-ai-send} asks for the provider and
model for that one send; otherwise it follows
@code{cmacs-ai-send-provider} and @code{cmacs-ai-send-model}, which
default to nil, meaning @code{cmacs-ai-default-provider} and
@code{cmacs-ai-default-model}.

The answer streams in (@code{cmacs-ai-send-stream}) but is recorded as a
single undo step either way, so one @kbd{C-/} removes a whole response.
@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-send-cancel} aborts everything still streaming into the
current buffer; killing the buffer does the same.

@node CMacs AI Generic Calls
@section Generic Calls

For scripting and user configs, @code{cmacs-ai-call} makes a one-shot
AI call from Elisp and returns the answer as a string -- with or
without tools.  Gate on @code{IS-CMACS-AI} if the config must also run
on a build without @option{--with-cmacs-ai}.

@example
;; Plain call, returns a string.
(cmacs-ai-call "Summarise this in one line: ...")
;; Agentic call with the built-in tools; the final answer is returned.
(cmacs-ai-call "What files are in my home directory?" :builtin-tools t)
@end example

@code{cmacs-ai-call} takes a plist: @code{:provider} (symbol),
@code{:system}, @code{:model}, @code{:max-tokens}, @code{:tools} (tool
specs), @code{:builtin-tools}, @code{:mcp-bridge} (needs
@option{--with-cmacs-mcp}), @code{:search-provider}, and
@code{:directory} (@pxref{CMacs AI Tool Use}).  Any tool
option builds a throwaway executor, uses it, and frees it.  It signals
@code{cmacs-ai-error} on failure.

@code{cmacs-ai-define-tool} builds a tool spec whose Elisp callback's
@emph{return value} is handed back to the model.  The callback is
@code{(funcall CALLBACK NAME INPUT-JSON ID)} and must return a string
(nil signals a tool error).  The executor runs synchronously on the
main thread, so callbacks may safely touch Emacs state.  Under the hood
@code{cmacs-ai-call} wraps the C primitive @code{cmacs-ai--call}; the
no-tools path equals @code{cmacs-ai-prompt-sync}.

@subsection From C, cmacsgi, D-Bus, emacsctl, and MCP

The calls are DEFUNs, so C and any eval-bridge client can drive them
(@code{cmacsgi eval '(cmacs-ai-call "...")'} works unchanged).  Typed
verbs also exist: @code{emacsctl ai prompt} and @code{emacsctl ai call}
(with @option{-t/--tools} for the built-in agent tools); the D-Bus
methods @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Ai.Prompt (ssss)} and
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Ai.Call (ssssb)}; and the MCP tools
@code{ai_prompt} and @code{ai_call}.  External callers cannot register
Elisp tools, so their tool-enabled call uses the server-side built-in
executor.

@quotation Warning
A tool-enabled call runs a synchronous multi-turn loop (capped at ~20
turns) on the target's main thread.  Under @command{emacs --gowl} that
thread is the Wayland compositor -- a long loop freezes the desktop.
Keep external tool loops short, and prefer @code{ai prompt} (no tools)
when driving a live @option{--gowl} session.
@end quotation

@node CMacs AI Inline Completion
@section Inline Ghost-Text Completion

@code{cmacs-ai-completion-mode} is a minor mode that drives idle-
timer-based fill-in-the-middle completion.  After
@code{cmacs-ai-completion-idle-delay} seconds of no input, a request
is fired with the buffer prefix + suffix as FIM context; the result
renders as a @code{shadow}-faced after-string overlay.  @kbd{TAB}
accepts; any other key dismisses.

Default provider is @code{claude-code} (CLI wrapper) so per-keystroke
HTTP costs are avoided; override with
@code{cmacs-ai-completion-provider}.

@node CMacs AI Voice Loop
@section Voice Loop

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-voice-chat} opens a chat buffer wired for full
voice round-trip: whisper transcribes a held-down recording via
@code{cmacs-whisper-dictate}, ai-glib streams the reply, and
@code{cmacs-piper-speak-region} speaks each completed sentence as
it streams in.  All four subsystems (@code{cmacs-whisper},
@code{cmacs-piper}, @code{cmacs-audio}, @code{cmacs-ai}) must be
enabled.

@table @code
@item cmacs-ai-voice-auto-speak
Whether replies are spoken.  Default @code{t}.
@end table

@node CMacs AI Coding Agent
@section Coding Agent

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-agent-open} opens (or resumes) the agent chat
for the current project root.  Before the first turn, it builds a
project-aware system prompt:

@itemize
@item
The first matching file from @code{cmacs-ai-agent-project-files}
(default: CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, README.org, README.md) is read in
full and attached as ``Project agent contract''.
@item
The absolute project root path is included.
@end itemize

This makes the assistant honor the project's own conventions
without manual cut-and-paste.

@node CMacs AI Tool Use
@section Tool Use

@code{cmacs-ai-tools-new} returns an @code{AiToolExecutor} handle
with all of ai-glib's built-ins pre-registered:

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{bash} -- run a shell command
@item @code{read} / @code{write} / @code{edit} -- file I/O
@item @code{glob} / @code{grep} / @code{ls} -- file discovery
@item @code{web_fetch} -- fetch a URL (HTML converted to text;
optional @code{prompt} extraction)
@end itemize

Per the project default, all tools are enabled (full trust).
Set @code{cmacs-ai-tool-confirm} to @code{'destructive} for a
prompt before bash/write/edit, or to a function for per-call
custom approval.

@code{web_fetch} is model-aware: HTML responses are converted to
readable markdown-style text (scripts and styles stripped, headings
and links preserved; images surface as Markdown @code{![alt](URL)}
with the src resolved to an absolute URL, so the model can cite or
embed real image links), @code{http://} is upgraded to @code{https://},
a @code{User-Agent} and a 30-second timeout are applied, and converted
bodies are cached in-process for 15 minutes.  With an optional
@code{prompt} argument, a sub-model reads the fetched page (through the
chat's active provider) and returns only the requested information
instead of the whole page.

@subsection Where the tools run

The built-in tools resolve relative paths against the executor's working
directory, and @code{bash} runs its command there.

@defun cmacs-ai-tools-set-working-directory executor directory
Point @var{executor}'s built-in tools at @var{directory}.  @code{nil}
restores the default, which is the Emacs process's own working
directory---for a long-lived editor, wherever you last visited a file,
and almost never what the model meant by @file{src/main.c}.  An absolute
path from the model is always used as given.

@code{cmacs-ai-call} takes the same thing as @code{:directory}.
Underneath it is ai-glib's
@code{ai_tool_executor_set_working_directory}; @code{bash} spawns
through @code{GSubprocess} rather than @code{popen} so the directory can
be set at all, which also makes the command cancellable and keeps its
output valid UTF-8.
@end defun

@subsection Custom Elisp tools

Custom Elisp tools are registered via
@code{cmacs-ai-tools-register}; the callback is invoked with
@code{(NAME INPUT-JSON ID)} on each model call and must return a string.

Callbacks may safely touch buffers and Emacs state.  On the synchronous
@code{cmacs-ai-call} path they run on the main thread directly; on the
background path (@code{cmacs-ai-tools-run-async}, which is what an
in-process brigade agent uses) the tool loop runs on a worker thread, so
the call is marshalled to the main thread and waited for.  It has to be:
Emacs's allocator is not thread-safe, so every @code{Lisp_Object}
involved must be built where Lisp lives, and only C strings cross the
boundary.

The run's own messages---the assistant's turns and the results of any
tools it called---are appended back onto the session before the callback
fires.  That is what lets the same session be run again as a
continuation rather than a restart: without it ai-glib's executor copies
the message list, appends to its copy and frees it, so a second run would
show the model its original instruction and the new question with no
trace of what it had already said or done.

Because every turn re-sends the whole session, a long conversation costs
quadratically in the number of turns, and does it quietly.

@defopt cmacs-ai-history-limit
Most messages a session keeps before the oldest are dropped (200).  The
oldest end is both the part the model is least likely to still need and
the only part safe to lose: standing instructions live in the client's
system prompt, not in this list, so they are never what gets dropped.
Zero or negative keeps everything, which is the right setting only if you
are watching the cost yourself.
@end defopt

@code{cmacs-ai-session-append-message} takes only @code{user} and
@code{assistant}.  There is no @code{system} role: a system prompt
belongs to the client, and is set with
@code{cmacs-ai-client-set-system-prompt}.

@defopt cmacs-ai-tool-call-timeout
Seconds a background tool loop waits for the main thread (120).  The
main thread picks the call up from its GLib dispatch, which happens
whenever Emacs reaches the event loop, so in practice this is immediate;
if it is not, the agent gets a timeout error for that one tool call
rather than the worker parking for the rest of the session.  Raise it if
you have Elisp tools that legitimately take minutes.
@end defopt

In addition to ai-glib's built-ins, every chat buffer is augmented
with cmacs's own MCP tool surface (buffer / file / project / eval /
apropos / describe by default).  See @ref{CMacs AI MCP Bridge}.

@subsection Web Search

@code{web_search} returns ranked results (title, URL, source domain,
snippet) and can optionally fetch the top results' pages.  Unlike
@code{web_fetch} it is not a free built-in: it is registered on a
chat/agent buffer's executor only when a backend is configured.
@code{cmacs-ai-chat--init} does this automatically from
@code{cmacs-ai-search-provider} (default @code{'auto}):

@table @code
@item auto
Brave or Bing when its key is set, else keyless DuckDuckGo.
@item brave
Brave Search (needs a key).
@item bing
Bing Search (needs a key).
@item duckduckgo
Keyless DuckDuckGo (best-effort, no SLA).
@item nil
Disabled.
@end table

Keyed backends read @code{cmacs-ai-search-api-key}, falling back to the
@code{BRAVE_API_KEY} / @code{BING_API_KEY} environment variable.  A keyed
provider with no key available logs a message and leaves @code{web_search}
off; @code{auto} always succeeds thanks to the keyless fallback.

Once registered, the model may use the tool's options: @code{count},
@code{freshness} (any/day/week/month/year), @code{safesearch}
(off/moderate/strict), @code{country}, @code{language}, @code{site}, and
@code{fetch_content} (fetch and attach the top results' page text via the
@code{web_fetch} pipeline).  Results are cached in-process for five
minutes.  Switch the backend for an open chat at runtime with
@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-chat-set-search-provider}; inspect the active tool set
with @code{(cmacs-ai-tools-list @var{executor})}.

@subsection Inline Images

When @code{cmacs-ai-chat-inline-images} is non-nil (the default), image
links in an assistant response render inline after each turn.  Have the
model embed images as bare Org links, e.g.@:
@code{[[https://host/pic.png]]} (a described link @code{[[url][text]]}
shows the text, not the image).  Local/file images preview synchronously
via Org; remote @code{http(s)} images are fetched @emph{asynchronously}
and shown when they arrive, so a slow or dead URL never blocks Emacs
(Org's own remote-image download is synchronous and would freeze the UI).
Non-image responses, error statuses, and undecodable data are ignored
silently.  Width is capped at @code{cmacs-ai-chat-image-max-width} (or
~92% of the chat window); @kbd{C-c C-x C-v} (@code{org-link-preview})
clears the previews.

@node CMacs AI Image Generation
@section Image Generation

Generate an image and put it where you are working.  In an Org buffer the
image is attached to the current node, linked, and previewed inline; the
same machinery is reachable from an @code{ai-image} babel block, from the
chat buffer, and over MCP / D-Bus / @command{emacsctl}.

@menu
* Image Quick Start::
* Image Providers and Capabilities::
* Image Storage::
* Image Commands::
* Image Parameters::
* Image Reference Conditioning::
* Image Babel Blocks::
* Image Chat Integration::
* Image Remote Surfaces::
* Image Troubleshooting::
* Image Low-Level API::
@end menu

@node Image Quick Start
@subsection Quick Start

With point in an Org buffer, under a heading:

@example
M-x cmacs-ai-image RET a brass telescope on a wooden desk RET
@end example

A moment later the buffer contains a @code{#+CAPTION:} recording the
prompt and an @code{[[attachment:...]]} link, rendered inline.  An
@code{:ID:} property appears on the heading: that is how Org locates the
attachment directory.

The bytes live in the node's @code{org-attach} directory, so the figure
travels with the node, is found by export, and survives the file being
moved.

Generation is asynchronous --- Emacs stays responsive for the tens of
seconds these take.

@node Image Providers and Capabilities
@subsection Providers, Models and Capabilities

Only three providers generate images.  Claude and Ollama expose no image
API at all, and asking them says so rather than failing obscurely.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .46 .36
@headitem Provider @tab Models @tab Reference images
@item Gemini
@tab Nano Banana, Nano Banana 2, Nano Banana Pro, Imagen 4 / 3
@tab up to 14 (Pro); none (Imagen)
@item OpenAI
@tab GPT Image 2 / 1.5 / 1, DALL-E 3 / 2
@tab up to 16 (GPT Image); 1 + mask (DALL-E 2)
@item Grok
@tab Grok Imagine, Grok Imagine Quality, Grok 2 Image
@tab 1 (Imagine); none (Grok 2)
@end multitable

Models differ in far more than that.  @kbd{M-x
cmacs-ai-image-list-models} opens a buffer listing each model's limits,
geometry vocabulary and capabilities; @code{cmacs-ai-image-models}
returns the same thing as data.

Options a model cannot honour are dropped rather than sent and rejected.
Two things are never dropped silently, because doing so would change the
result invisibly: reference images beyond a model's limit, and an
unsupported operation.

@node Image Storage
@subsection Where Images Are Stored

In Org, @code{org-attach} takes ownership.  If the node has no @code{ID}
or @code{DIR} property yet, one is created.

Everywhere else --- a non-Org buffer, a read-only one, or a position
before the first heading, where there is no node to attach to --- the
image goes to @code{cmacs-ai-image-dir} (default
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/cmacs-ai/images/}) and a bare path is inserted
instead of a link.  A message says which happened.

@code{cmacs-ai-image-attach-method} controls whether the file is moved
(default) or copied.  Generated images start in a temporary file, so
moving is both correct and cheaper.

@node Image Commands
@subsection Commands

@table @code
@item cmacs-ai-image
Generate from a prompt, or the active region.  With a prefix argument,
ask for provider, model and options --- but only the options the chosen
model honours, so there is no way to interactively request something
that would be silently dropped.
@item cmacs-ai-image-menu
A transient for composing a request option by option.  It is
capability-aware: every option is offered against the model actually
selected --- aspect ratios from that model's own list, quality words
from its own vocabulary --- and options the model cannot honour are
hidden rather than shown and silently dropped.  Reference images
accumulate, as many as the model accepts, each with an optional role.
Emptying a prompt clears that option, returning it to the model's
default.
@item cmacs-ai-image-from-selected-text
Illustrate the selected text, inserting the image just after it.  The
selection is the prompt and is left in place.  Unlike
@code{cmacs-ai-image}, which inserts at point --- and point sits at
whichever end of the region you finished selecting at --- this always
puts the image below the text.  The insertion point is held with a
marker, so it stays correct even if you keep editing elsewhere while the
image generates.
@item cmacs-ai-image-edit
Generate conditioned on reference images.
@item cmacs-ai-image-regenerate
Re-run the image at point from its @code{#+CAPTION}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-variations
Several images for one prompt.
@item cmacs-ai-image-save-as
Copy the image at point elsewhere.
@item cmacs-ai-image-list-models
Show what each model supports.
@end table

Suggested bindings:

@example
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c a i RET") #'cmacs-ai-image-menu)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c a i i") #'cmacs-ai-image)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c a i s") #'cmacs-ai-image-from-selected-text)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c a i e") #'cmacs-ai-image-edit)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c a i r") #'cmacs-ai-image-regenerate)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c a i m") #'cmacs-ai-image-list-models)
@end example

@node Image Parameters
@subsection Parameters

@code{cmacs-ai-image-generate-async} takes an options plist covering the
whole request surface.  Omitting a key leaves the provider on its own
default rather than on ours.

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@headitem Group @tab Keys
@item Prompt @tab @code{:prompt} @code{:negative} @code{:system}
@item Operation @tab @code{:operation} (generate / edit / variation / upscale)
@item Geometry @tab @code{:size} @code{:custom-size} @code{:aspect} @code{:resolution}
@item Appearance @tab @code{:quality} @code{:style} @code{:style-preset} @code{:background}
@item Output @tab @code{:format} @code{:compression} @code{:response-format}
@item Sampling @tab @code{:seed} @code{:guidance} @code{:steps} @code{:strength} @code{:temperature} @code{:top-p} @code{:top-k}
@item Policy @tab @code{:moderation} @code{:person-generation} @code{:watermark} @code{:enhance-prompt} @code{:language}
@item Editing @tab @code{:references} @code{:mask} @code{:fidelity} @code{:partial-images}
@item Escape @tab @code{:extra}
@end multitable

Geometry is expressed two incompatible ways: the Gemini family thinks in
aspect ratios plus a resolution tier, OpenAI in pixel dimensions.  Both
are carried, and each provider uses whichever applies.

@code{:extra} is a plist spliced verbatim into the request body, for
parameters newer than this binding.  Nothing in it is validated.

@node Image Reference Conditioning
@subsection Reference Images and Editing

Several input images, each doing a different job:

@example
(cmacs-ai-image-edit
 "combine these into a concert poster"
 '(("palette.png" . "style")
   ("macaw.jpg"   . "subject")
   ("venue.jpg"   . "background")))
@end example

Interactively, references come from the image at point, from the marked
files in Dired, or are read one at a time; a prefix argument also asks
for a role for each.

Order is preserved and significant.  Roles matter because no wire format
has a field for them --- ai-glib folds the labels into the prompt text,
which is the only channel the model reads.  Unlabelled references are
passed through in order.

For a masked edit, the transparent areas of the mask are the ones
regenerated.

@node Image Babel Blocks
@subsection The ai-image Babel Block

Lets a figure live in the document that describes it:

@example
#+BEGIN_SRC ai-image :provider gemini :aspect 16:9 :resolution 2k
A hyacinth macaw perched on a brass telescope, cinematic lighting
#+END_SRC
@end example

@kbd{C-c C-c} generates and puts the link in @code{#+RESULTS:}, so
re-evaluating regenerates the figure in place.  Register the language
first with @code{(cmacs-ai-image-block-register)}.

Header args mirror the parameter list, plus @code{:file} to write
somewhere explicit instead of attaching, and @code{:ref} (repeatable,
@code{PATH} or @code{PATH::ROLE}).

This path is @emph{synchronous} --- org-babel has no async protocol ---
so it blocks for as long as generation takes.
@code{cmacs-ai-image-timeout} (default 180 s) bounds the wait.

@node Image Chat Integration
@subsection Chat Integration

@code{/image PROMPT} in the compose region generates one directly, with
no model turn involved.

Slash commands are a hook, @code{cmacs-ai-chat-slash-command-functions}:
each function sees the compose line and returns non-nil when it consumed
it.  The second brain adds @code{/ingest} --- @code{/ingest URL},
@code{/ingest PATH}, @code{/ingest -p resources -c technical/linux URL},
@code{/ingest -- some text}, or a bare @code{/ingest} (also @kbd{C-c
C-b}) to file the conversation itself.  @xref{Secondbrain Ingest}.

The @code{generate_image} tool lets the model decide to draw something
mid-conversation.  It is on by default; set
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-enable-image-tool} to @code{nil} to withhold it.

The tool is deliberately @emph{not} named @code{ai_generate_image}.  The
MCP bridge hides every tool matching @code{^ai_} from in-process chat
buffers to stop the model calling itself recursively, and an in-process
chat buffer is exactly where this one has to be visible.

@node Image Remote Surfaces
@subsection MCP, D-Bus and emacsctl

@example
emacsctl ai generate-image -p gemini -a 16:9 "a brass telescope"

emacsctl ai generate-image \
  -m gemini-3-pro-image-preview \
  -r logo.png::style -r subject.jpg::subject \
  -b notes.org "combine these into a poster"
@end example

The MCP tool is @code{generate_image}; the D-Bus method is
@code{GenerateImage} on @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Ai}.  All three block the
cmacs main thread for the duration.

References travel as a single comma-separated @code{PATH} or
@code{PATH::ROLE} string on all three surfaces, because the MCP bridge's
schema translator cannot carry a nested list.

@node Image Troubleshooting
@subsection Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item ``provider does not support image generation''
You asked Claude or Ollama.  Use gemini, openai or grok.
@item ``Authentication failed''
The provider's key is missing from the environment:
@env{GEMINI_API_KEY}, @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}, @env{XAI_API_KEY}.
@item The model answered with text
Surfaces as an invalid-response error rather than an empty success.
Usually the prompt was declined; rephrasing helps.
@item A content-policy refusal
Arrives as an invalid-request error with the provider's own explanation
quoted, which is the useful part.
@item ``attaching failed; using @dots{}''
There was no Org node to attach to.  The image was saved to
@code{cmacs-ai-image-dir} instead; nothing was lost.
@item An option seemed to be ignored
It probably was, because the model does not support it.
@code{cmacs-ai-image-list-models} says which does what.
@end table

@node Image Low-Level API
@subsection Low-Level API

@table @code
@item cmacs-ai-image-generate-async
Generate; returns a job handle.
@item cmacs-ai-image-generate-sync
Blocking variant, for org-babel.
@item cmacs-ai-image-cancel
Abandon a job by handle.
@item cmacs-ai-image-models
Per-model capabilities and limits.
@end table

The async callback receives

@example
(:images ((:data BYTES :mime M :url U :base64 B :revised R) ...))
@end example

or @code{(:error MSG)}.  @code{BYTES} is a unibyte string, already
materialised whether the provider returned inline data or a URL --- so a
caller can write it straight to a file without knowing which.

@node CMacs AI Org Blocks
@section Org Babel Integration

@code{cmacs-ai-org-block-register} adds an @code{ai} language to
@code{org-babel-load-languages}:

@example
#+BEGIN_SRC ai :provider claude :system "Be brief"
Explain the GIL.
#+END_SRC
@end example

@kbd{C-c C-c} on the block sends the body and inserts the response
in @code{#+RESULTS:}.

@node CMacs AI GObject Introspection
@section GObject Introspection

The cmacs-ai init path prepends @code{$top_srcdir/deps/ai-glib/build/release}
to GIRepository's search path and eagerly requires @code{AiGlib-1.0}.
After @code{init_cmacs_ai} runs, the typelib is reachable from:

@itemize @bullet
@item Elisp: @code{(gi-require "AiGlib" "1.0")} returns @code{t}
without any manual @env{GI_TYPELIB_PATH} setup.
@item The bacon @code{cmacsgi} builtin: @code{cmacsgi eval "(gi-require
\"AiGlib\" \"1.0\")"}.
@item The MCP @code{gi_*} tools (@code{gi_list_namespaces},
@code{gi_describe}, @code{gi_call}).
@end itemize

This is the same machinery used for all other GI-exposed cmacs
libraries; no per-class binding code is needed.

@node CMacs AI MCP Tools
@section MCP Tools

Built with @option{--with-cmacs-mcp}, six tools are exposed on the
cmacs MCP socket:

@table @code
@item ai_prompt
One-shot synchronous prompt.  Args: @code{prompt} (required),
@code{provider}, @code{system}, @code{model}.
@item ai_call
Like @code{ai_prompt}, plus a @code{tools} boolean: when true the model
is given the built-in agent tools (bash/read/write/edit/glob/grep/ls/
web_fetch) and runs a synchronous multi-turn loop.  The loop blocks the
cmacs main thread for its duration.
@item ai_list_providers
Enumerate provider symbols.
@item ai_list_models
List the model names each provider offers, as a JSON object mapping
provider to a model-name array (network for API providers, static
tables for CLI providers).  Optional @code{provider} restricts the
query; otherwise all providers are queried and unavailable ones
report inline instead of failing the call.
@item ai_open_chat
Open a chat buffer (optionally seeded with a first prompt; optional
@code{model} overrides the provider's default).  Returns the buffer
name.
@item generate_image
Generate an image and insert it into a cmacs buffer.  Args:
@code{prompt} (required), @code{references} (comma-separated
@code{PATH} or @code{PATH::ROLE}), @code{provider}, @code{model},
@code{aspect}, @code{size}, @code{quality}, @code{buffer}.  Blocks the
cmacs main thread for the tens of seconds generation takes.  Named
without the @code{ai_} prefix on purpose --- see @ref{Image Chat
Integration}.
@end table

External agents (e.g. an outer Claude Code instance) can drive the
in-editor AI through these tools.  The underlying DEFUNs are
@code{cmacs-ai-prompt-sync} (@var{prompt} @code{&optional}
@var{provider} @var{system} @var{model}), @code{cmacs-ai--call} (the
tools-capable primitive behind @code{cmacs-ai-call}), and
@code{cmacs-ai-list-models} (@code{&optional} @var{provider});
D-Bus parity lives on @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Ai}
(@code{ListProviders} / @code{ListModels} / @code{Prompt} /
@code{Call} / @code{OpenChat}).

@node CMacs AI MCP Bridge
@section MCP Bridge

The previous section described the @emph{outbound} MCP surface ---
@code{ai_*} tools that external agents can call into cmacs to drive
the in-editor AI.  This section covers the @emph{inbound} mirror:
each chat buffer's @code{AiToolExecutor} is automatically augmented
with cmacs's own MCP tool surface, so the in-process AI gets the
same editor-aware tools (buffer / file / project / eval / apropos /
describe) that external Claude Code already uses to drive cmacs.

Without this bridge, the model only sees ai-glib's filesystem-level
built-ins (@code{bash}, @code{read}, @code{write}, @code{edit},
@code{glob}, @code{grep}, @code{ls}, @code{web_fetch}).  Those work,
but edits land on disk bypassing cmacs buffers (no undo, no
save-hooks, races with unsaved user work), and searches don't use
cmacs's project boundaries.  The bridge solves this by exposing
cmacs's editor-grade tools alongside ai-glib's built-ins ---
augmenting rather than replacing.

@subsection Default allowlist

The default set covers the read-only introspection tools plus the
four safe-edit mutators that respect buffer invariants:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Eval + introspection}: @code{eval}, @code{describe_function},
@code{describe_variable}, @code{apropos}, @code{completions}
@item
@strong{Buffer surface}: @code{list_buffers}, @code{get_buffer_content},
@code{set_buffer_content}, @code{create_buffer}, @code{edit_buffer},
@code{replace_in_buffer}, @code{search_buffer}, @code{goto_line}
@item
@strong{Window introspection}: @code{list_windows}, @code{list_frames}
@item
@strong{Project surface} (workspace-confined): @code{project_root},
@code{project_read_file}, @code{project_list_files},
@code{project_find_files}, @code{project_grep}
@item
@strong{Process + debug read-only}: @code{list_processes},
@code{backtrace}, @code{memory_info}, @code{process_status},
@code{recent_messages}, @code{describe_mode}, @code{list_hooks}
@end itemize

When the model invokes one of these, the call goes through the SAME
handler an external MCP client would hit (via the new
@code{mcp_server_invoke_tool} entry point), then the result string is
handed back to ai-glib as the @code{tool_result}.  The chat buffer
renders the call and its result as @code{***} subsections under the
@code{**} assistant turn, identical to ai-glib built-in tool calls.

@subsection Opting tools in or out

Allowlist patterns are PCRE regexps (the bridge runs through GLib's
GRegex) --- @emph{not} Emacs' @code{\`X\'} syntax.  To add shell
access plus the GObject Introspection surface, append to
@code{cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-allowlist}:

@example
(add-to-list 'cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-allowlist "^bacon_eval$")
(add-to-list 'cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-allowlist "^crispy_eval$")
(add-to-list 'cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-allowlist "^gi_")
@end example

To gate a single chat buffer to read-only tools:

@example
(setq-local cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-readonly-only t)
@end example

before opening it.

@subsection Recursion guard

The C layer always denies @code{^ai_} (the outbound MCP tool names),
regardless of what you put in @code{cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-allowlist}.
This prevents the in-process AI from looping by invoking
@code{ai_open_chat} or @code{ai_prompt} on itself.  This guard is
not configurable.

@subsection Trust

Bridged MCP tools obey @code{cmacs-ai-tool-confirm} exactly like the
ai-glib built-ins documented under @ref{CMacs AI Tool Use}.  One
knob, symmetric semantics: @code{nil} for full trust (the
project-wide default), @code{'destructive} to prompt before any tool
that lacks the MCP read-only hint, or a function for arbitrary
per-call gating.

@node CMacs AI Configuration
@section Configuration

@table @code
@item cmacs-ai-default-provider
Default provider symbol.  Default @code{claude}.
@item cmacs-ai-default-model
Default model string, or @code{nil} for the provider default.
@item cmacs-ai-system-prompt
System prompt for new chat sessions.  Besides the coding-assistant
boilerplate it states the conventions of the tools cmacs ships, which a
model cannot infer from a tool schema.  Chiefly: @code{agent_spawn} is
asynchronous and @strong{must not be polled}---it returns an id at once
and the brigade loopback delivers a message into the conversation when
the agent finishes, so a model that has not been told sits in an
@code{agent_status} loop until it gives up.  Also that only the tools in
its list exist, and that the editor it is acting on is live.
@item cmacs-ai-max-tokens
Default max output tokens.  Default @code{4096}.
@item cmacs-ai-enabled-tools
Built-in tools advertised to the model.
@item cmacs-ai-tool-confirm
@code{nil} (never prompt) / @code{'destructive} / function.
@item cmacs-ai-image-provider
Provider used for image generation.  Default @code{gemini}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-model
Image model, or @code{nil} for the provider default.
@item cmacs-ai-image-aspect
Default aspect ratio, e.g. @code{"16:9"} (Gemini family).
@item cmacs-ai-image-size
Default pixel size, e.g. @code{"1024x1024"} (OpenAI family).
@item cmacs-ai-image-resolution
Default resolution tier: @code{"1k"}, @code{"2k"} or @code{"4k"}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-count
Images per generation.  Default @code{1}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-quality
Default quality; translated to whichever vocabulary the model accepts.
@item cmacs-ai-image-background
@code{"transparent"} or @code{"opaque"}; needs a format with alpha.
@item cmacs-ai-image-format
Output encoding: @code{"png"}, @code{"jpeg"} or @code{"webp"}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-negative
Default negative prompt.
@item cmacs-ai-image-dir
Where images go when they cannot be attached to an Org node.  Default
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/cmacs-ai/images/}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-attach-method
@code{mv} (default) or @code{cp}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-file-name-format
@code{format-time-string} pattern for generated names.
@item cmacs-ai-image-insert-caption
Insert a @code{#+CAPTION} recording the prompt.  Default @code{t}; this
is what @code{cmacs-ai-image-regenerate} reads back.
@item cmacs-ai-image-preview
Preview inline after inserting.  Default @code{t}.
@item cmacs-ai-image-timeout
Seconds a synchronous generation may take.  Default @code{180}; applies
to the org-babel path only.
@item cmacs-ai-chat-enable-image-tool
Offer the model a @code{generate_image} tool in chat.  Default
@code{t}.
@item cmacs-ai-search-provider
Backend for the @code{web_search} tool: @code{auto} (default),
@code{brave}, @code{bing}, @code{duckduckgo}, or @code{nil} (disabled).
@item cmacs-ai-search-api-key
API key for Brave/Bing, or @code{nil} to read @code{BRAVE_API_KEY} /
@code{BING_API_KEY} from the environment.
@item cmacs-ai-chat-inline-images
Render image links in assistant responses inline (default @code{t});
remote images are fetched asynchronously.
@item cmacs-ai-chat-image-max-width
Max inline image width in pixels (default 800).
@item cmacs-ai-chat-dir
Where archived chats land; also the pool that
@code{cmacs-ai-resume-chat} lists.
@item cmacs-ai-chat-autosave
Archive chats automatically on send, on each response, and on
buffer-kill (default @code{t}).  When @code{nil}, only the manual
@kbd{C-c C-s} save remains.
@item cmacs-ai-chat-save-name-format
File-name template for archives: a @code{format-time-string} pattern
whose @code{<provider>} token is replaced with the provider name.
Default @code{%y%m%d-%H%M%S-<provider>.org}.
@item cmacs-ai-config-file
Path to ai-glib's optional YAML config
(@file{~/.config/ai-glib/config.yaml} by default).  Honored by
ai-glib's @code{AiConfig} singleton; this defcustom is
informational only.
@end table

API keys are sourced from environment variables (see Providers
above) or from @file{~/.config/ai-glib/config.yaml}.
